<h2>How FIRs acquire new offences</h2>
<p>Nothing in law freezes an FIR at its registered sections. The investigating officer applies and revises provisions as material emerges: medical reports upgrade hurt to grievous hurt; a victim&rsquo;s deteriorating condition or death transforms the case; documents seized convert a money dispute into forgery; call records introduce conspiracy. Under the BNS-BNSS framework, as under the IPC-CrPC regime before it, the test at every stage is the material in the case diary, and the court takes cognizance on the police report&rsquo;s final array of offences — not the FIR&rsquo;s first draft.</p>

<h2>The legal position in outline</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Protection is accusation-specific.</strong> A pre-arrest bail order records the offences considered. Its reasoning — gravity, punishment, custodial need — is tied to them.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Cognate additions: usually covered.</strong> Where added sections arise from the same transaction and carry comparable gravity, courts treat the earlier assessment as substantially holding.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Material aggravation: fresh assessment needed.</strong> Where the added offence changes the case&rsquo;s character — far higher punishment, or a special-statute bar on bail — the safer and accepted course is a fresh judicial look, sought by the accused or forced by the prosecution.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Statutory bars operate immediately.</strong> If the added offence attracts a provision restricting anticipatory bail itself, the earlier order cannot do service for an offence the court may have had no power to protect against.</div>
</div>

<h2>A worked illustration</h2>
<p>Consider a matrimonial FIR under Section 85 BNS (cruelty) and Section 115(2) (voluntarily causing hurt), in which the husband obtains anticipatory bail. Months later, on the complainant&rsquo;s supplementary statement, the police add Section 308 extortion allegations against him and a dowry-death provision against his parents after the complainant&rsquo;s suicide attempt is re-characterised. The husband&rsquo;s original protection was granted on a cruelty-and-hurt case; the case now carries offences of a very different order. If he waits, he risks arrest on the new sections with the argument that the old order never addressed them; if he moves a fresh application, he chooses his forum, frames the first narrative on the additions, and can seek interim protection for the gap.</p>

<h2>Practice architecture for the defence</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Monitor the case diary stage-wise: applications for certified copies of orders on remand/notice stages, and police communications, often reveal additions before any formal intimation reaches the accused.</p>
  <p>On learning of added sections, obtain the current FIR/chargesheet array in writing — argue from the record, not from rumour.</p>
  <p>File the fresh Section 482 BNSS application with complete candour about the earlier order; suppression is the one error courts do not forgive in bail jurisdiction.</p>
  <p>Address the added offences head-on: why the new material does not prima facie implicate the applicant, and why custodial interrogation adds nothing — the same discipline that won the first order.</p>
  <p>Where the addition looks tactical — engineered to defeat the bail — say so with specifics: timing, absence of new material, and the investigating officer&rsquo;s own earlier stand.</p>
</div>

<h2>For complainants and the State</h2>
<p>The mirror lesson holds. Where genuinely graver material surfaces, the prosecution should place it before the court that granted protection rather than attempt arrest by surprise; an arrest effected in the teeth of a subsisting order invites judicial displeasure even when the legal position favours the State. The clean route — a cancellation or clarification application, served on the accused — protects the investigation and the eventual trial record alike.</p>
<div class="note">This explainer states general principles of bail practice for information only. The effect of added offences on an existing protection order is fact-specific and forum-specific, and urgent advice should be taken in any live situation.</div>
